{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792602359,"nonce":"HdQRnugVr0nmDTohv5khw70qy9Q=","message":"The phase profile found one avoidable shared runtime cost: TieredBackend eagerly created LLVM's host TargetMachine while every program still begins interpreted. Move host registration, LLVM option parsing and TargetMachine construction to the first LLVM IR compilation, before IR construction; retain the machine in a per-backend OnceCell for subsequent entries/resume points. Symbol registration, module context and optimization settings do not force target creation. Interpreter -\u003e Cranelift -\u003e LLVM and the existing promotion/OSR policies are unchanged. This shared compiler change applies to LLVM-selected Python/Lua/TS runtimes without frontend cache wiring.\n\nAdd ZYNODE phase traces through the existing ZYNTAX_TRACE_LOWER_PHASES flag and backend-construction subphases (Cranelift, LLVM setup, brokers). Enable the existing allocation-audit allocator/report in the zynode binary; extend the isolated allocation fixture to the closed execution-cache path as well as reusable open HIR caching. Document interpretation of nested timings in testing.md; no README journal added.\n\nDiagnosis: strace showed a tiny interpreter-only run still creates Beadie broker/promoter and compile-worker threads; their construction was ~0.06 ms, whereas LLVM setup was ~0.25-0.28 ms. Lazy setup reduced that phase to ~0.02 ms in randomized diagnostic samples. Native target setup is not the whole previous regression: --version already reproduced about 0.4 ms of the difference before constructing any runtime, alongside extra minor faults; relinking the instrumented executable moved the result again. Do not credit the entire wall-time change to deferred LLVM work or imply hot-loop throughput improved.\n\nFinal exact-source controlled NUC comparison, candidate versus 3deaedf43cc082ac015b838430a438013e8679f4's frozen work